# ============================================================================
# deploy/caddy/maintenance-server.caddy — the loopback static server for the maintenance page
# (B485; installed by deploy/install-maintenance-page.sh, imported at TOP LEVEL of the Caddyfile)
#
# Why a second listener instead of file_server inside handle_errors: measured on Caddy v2.11.9, a
# file_server inside an error handler answers 5xx with an EMPTY body (the handler fails and the error
# status is rendered again), while a plain reverse_proxy to this listener serves the page.
#
# STATUS 503, NOT 200. Measured the same day: a 200 maintenance page is a lie to every health checker
# — the release catalog's own B-prod-health parsed the HTML as if the panel were healthy, and any
# uptime monitor would have called a stopped panel "up". Browsers render a 503 body just fine (with
# Retry-After), so the page keeps its explanation while monitoring sees the outage it is.
#
# Bound to loopback only; it carries no secrets. The directory is /etc/caddy/maintenance on purpose:
# the panel's data dir /var/lib/skygate is 0750 skygate:skygate BY DESIGN, and the caddy service user
# could not traverse into it — the first install proved it by taking the site down with
# "Failed to import … permission denied".
# ============================================================================

http://127.0.0.1:8081 {
	root * /etc/caddy/maintenance
	rewrite * /index.html
	file_server {
		status 503
	}
}